--- a/guidance.md
+++ b/guidance.md
@@ -1 +1,25 @@
-Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+Spreadsheet work. `workbook_inspect`, `workbook_write`, `workbook_recalc`, `workbook_review` and `workbook_diff` operate on XLSX files. An independent engine recalculates the delivered file, so the deliverable must contain live formulas, not typed-in numbers.
+
+How to read the source before writing anything:
+
+- Inspect the whole workbook first. Read every comment. A comment states a scope rule for the cell or row it sits on; treat it as the binding definition of that cell, and re-read it before building a formula that uses the cell.
+- Use the label text as the definition of what a row is. Do not assume a row's role from its position. Classify each labelled row: a reported input, a component of a build-up, a rate, a total, or a ratio.
+- Check indentation and grouping. Source workbooks often indent the components of a build-up block and set peaks or totals at a wider indent. A block's components and its total are different things even when the labels look similar.
+- A rate applies only to the base it is defined on. If a comment restricts a rate to one adjustment, that rate must not multiply a total, and a rate must never be averaged into a metric.
+
+Building formulas:
+
+- Every requested output cell must be a formula that references the source cells, so that the engine recomputes it. Never paste the numeric result of your own arithmetic into a cell the task asks you to fill.
+- Build one column's formulas correctly, verify them, then extend across every displayed period column. A row that is filled for some periods and empty for others is a defect.
+- Follow the workbook's own arithmetic. If the source builds a total from components, your totals must reference those component cells, and derived metrics must reference the totals rather than re-derive them.
+- Match the workbook's existing direction and sign conventions for each component. Before trusting a sign, check it against the comment for that row, and confirm that the components of a single block move together as the block's inputs grow.
+- Respect intentionally blank cells. Cells the task says are blank, or that are blank in the source for a structural reason, stay blank. Never fill a cell that the task marks as intentionally empty, and never clear a cell you were not asked to change.
+- Use the workbook's own conventions for units, scale and number format. If margins and rates are stored as fractions, return fractions. Do not multiply by 100, do not change number formats, and do not add units or labels that are not asked for.
+- When a value is asked for in one period only, leave the other period cells blank instead of repeating or extrapolating.
+
+Before delivering:
+
+- Write to a copy, keeping every original input, label, comment and unit byte-identical.
+- Run `workbook_recalc` to confirm every formula evaluates to a number. A formula that returns nothing or an error is a defect.
+- Run `workbook_review` with the source workbook to check arithmetic consistency, sign conventions, partial rows and preserved inputs. Then run `workbook_diff` against the source and confirm that only the intended target cells changed.
+- Read your computed values back and sanity check their direction and magnitude against the source trends before you finish.
